      <h2>error messages and what they mean</h2>
      <div class="troubleshoot-list">
        <div class="troubleshoot-item">
          <h3>Domain already added to another Microsoft 365 organisation</h3>
          <p>Another tenant has verified the domain, and a domain can be verified in only one tenant at a time. The exact message varies by admin centre and language, and it may not say which tenant holds the domain, as in my case. Run the realm lookup and the OpenID lookup, or the <a href="/tools/domain-tenant-lookup">Domain Tenant Lookup</a>, to get the organisation name, the tenant ID and the cloud, then decide whether the tenant is managed or unmanaged.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="troubleshoot-item">
          <h3>Failed. Reason = Cannot delete the domain currently referenced by at least 1 objects. Conflicting object User/&lt;OBJECT_ID&gt;.</h3>
          <p>forceDelete was accepted but could not finish, because an object still references the domain. The object ID after <code>User/</code> is the account to deal with. In my case it was an unlicensed account whose primary SMTP address was on the domain, and my reading is that the Exchange precondition is what forceDelete hit. Rename the account off the domain, or delete it from the recycle bin as well if it is stale, then remove the domain.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="troubleshoot-item">
          <h3>Invalid cross-cloud verification code</h3>
          <p>Returned as HTTP 400 with <code>Request_BadRequest</code> when I tried external admin takeover with <code>forceTakeover</code> set to <code>true</code>, with the TXT record live in DNS and both tenants in the same cloud. Microsoft does not document this error. I saw it against a managed tenant, where takeover is not available, so release the domain from inside that tenant instead.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="troubleshoot-item">
          <h3>Domain has associated subdomains</h3>
          <p>Microsoft lists this as an error when removing a domain. A subdomain of the domain is still verified in the same tenant, so remove the subdomain first, then remove the parent domain.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

